<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Surat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Surat is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a major commercial centre under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. In 1664 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> raided it. Accounts of the Maratha Empire treat the raid as a demonstration of Maratha power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/surat.jpg" alt="Surat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rahul Bhadane · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bharthana_Althan_area.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Surat stands on the banks of the Tapti River, known popularly as the Tapi, close to its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. The city lies 284 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhinagar">Gandhinagar</a>, the state capital, 265 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> and 289 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>. The Gujarati, Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> word surat means countenance. After dams were built on the Tapi its water flow decreased and the original port facilities closed, so the nearest port is now in the Magadalla and Hazira area of the metropolitan region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The traditional account holds that Surat was built up around 1500 by a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> merchant named Gopi, who asked the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> to approve the name Suryapur, city of the sun. The Muslim king is said to have altered it to Surat, after the word surah, the name for the chapters of the Qur'an. Both names appear in sources before 1500, so the town and its name are older than Gopi's time. Before the 1500s the nearby town of Rander was the main commercial centre; it declined because of Portuguese raids, and some of its merchants probably moved to Surat.</p><p>Under the Mughal Empire Surat was the most prosperous port of the realm. Despite its wealth it looked like a typical trader's town of mud-and-bamboo tenements and crooked streets, with a few mansions and warehouses along the riverfront and the establishments of Turkish, Armenian, English, French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> traders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> established its first Indian trading factory there in 1612. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> looted the city twice, the first sacking coming in 1664, and his raids scared trade away. Under the British Raj Surat declined, remaining important for textile products but less important as a port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Surat is the commercial and economic centre of South Gujarat and the administrative capital of the Surat district. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave it a population of 4,467,797. Its diamond trade began in the late 1950s, when the first diamond workshops of Gujarat appeared in Surat and Navsari, and its textile industry is long established. Surat railway station was built in 1860.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Surat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It set up a factory at Masulipatnam in 1611, gained rights at <b>Surat</b> from the Mughal Emperor Jahangir in 1612, built a factory at Madras in 1640 and leased Bombay island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War Aurangzeb let it settle at Calcutta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Notable campaigns under Shivaji included the capture of Torna (1646), the killing of the Bijapur general Afzal Khan at Pratapgad (1659) and the raid on <b>Surat</b> (1664).</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th and 19th centuries European scholars, beginning with the Frenchman Abraham Hyacinthe Anquetil-Duperron, who studied with Parsi priests in <b>Surat</b> and published a translation of the Avesta in 1771, made the Zoroastrian scriptures known to the West.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Surat</b>, the empire’s great port, Virji Vora traded in pepper, bullion, coral and opium through agents from Agra to Calicut, lent to the English and Dutch companies, and was said by the English to be worth 8 million rupees.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the 1960s it has been sung in Timor-Leste as &quot;Ha'u Hakerek <b>Surat</b> Ida&quot;, with words by Momô dos Mártires, and its melody carries an Irish hymn to the Virgin Mary.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pokarekare-ana">Pōkarekare Ana</a></li><li><blockquote>The naval Battle of Swally, fought on 29 and 30 November off Suvali near <b>Surat</b>, ended in victory for four English East India Company galleons over four Portuguese galleons and 26 barks.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
